@page "/Home"

<MudText Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-4" Typo="Typo.h2">OSDC Earth Magnetic Field</MudText>
<MudDivider Class="mb-4" />
<MudText Class="mx-6 px-6" Typo="Typo.body1">
    This microservice performs stateless WMM2025 and IGRF14 calculations at WGS84 positions and UTC instants. Public
    API values always use SI: latitude and longitude are radians, and depth is metres positive downward from the
    WGS84 reference ellipsoid. The web interface can display and accept other units through the OSDC unit-reference
    system while preserving SI on the API boundary.
</MudText>

<MudGrid Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-2">
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Why the field varies by position</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Most of the Earth's magnetic field — roughly 95% of it at the surface — is generated by convective
                flow of liquid iron in the outer core, the <b>geodynamo</b>. This main field is not a simple dipole:
                its geomagnetic poles do not coincide with the geographic poles, and its strength and direction both
                depend strongly on latitude and longitude, from about 25,000 nT near the magnetic equator to over
                65,000 nT near the poles.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                A smaller crustal contribution, from magnetised rock in the Earth's crust, adds local anomalies on
                top of the smooth core field. WMM2025 and IGRF14 are main-field models and do not resolve those local
                anomalies. <b>Declination</b> — the angle between geodetic north and magnetic north — can differ by
                tens of degrees between locations, so a compass correction always depends on position.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Why the field varies with depth</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                WMM2025 and IGRF14 model the large-scale internal main field, which is dominated by the Earth's core.
                Their spherical-harmonic expansions can be evaluated above or slightly below the reference
                ellipsoid within each model's published height range, but they do not model magnetic interference
                from nearby formations, equipment, or other local sources.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                This service accepts SI <code>Depth</code> from -850 km to +1 km for WMM2025 and from -600 km to
                +1 km for IGRF14. Because depth is positive downward, the large negative limits represent altitude
                above the ellipsoid; both models permit only 1 km below it. The service converts depth to
                GeographicLib's positive-up ellipsoidal height convention only at the implementation boundary.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Why the field varies with time</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                The core flow that drives the geodynamo changes slowly, so the main field drifts year over year —
                <b>secular variation</b>. Declination at a fixed point can shift by a fraction of a degree annually,
                which is why compass-based surveys and directional drilling both need a field value for the actual
                date, not just the location.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                WMM uses an epoch field plus secular-variation coefficients for its five-year predictive lifetime.
                IGRF provides successive five-year main-field epochs back to 1900 and a predictive secular-variation
                interval at the current end of the series. Neither model captures the much faster disturbances from
                the ionosphere and magnetosphere (space weather); both target the slowly varying main field.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">How the field is estimated: WMM2025 and IGRF14</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                <b>WMM2025</b> (World Magnetic Model) is produced jointly by NOAA/NCEI and the British Geological
                Survey for the US National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency and the UK Defence Geographic Centre, and
                is a reference for military and civilian navigation systems. <b>IGRF14</b>
                (International Geomagnetic Reference Field), maintained by IAGA, extends back to 1900 and is the
                standard reference for scientific and geomagnetic work.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Both represent the internal field as a spherical-harmonic (Gauss coefficient) expansion, released in
                five-year epochs. This service loads both once at startup using <b>GeographicLib</b>'s
                implementation. Exact model identity, degree/order, release date, validity bounds, and a SHA-256 of
                each coefficient file are available on the
                <MudLink Href="/EarthMagneticField/webapp/EarthMagneticFieldModel">Magnetic models</MudLink> page and
                via the model-information API and MCP tool.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
</MudGrid>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">OSDC convention</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Latitude and longitude are always WGS84 geodetic coordinates in SI radians. <code>Depth</code> is SI metres,
        positive downward from the WGS84 reference ellipsoid — the same depth reference used across OSDC's Earth
        microservices, never mean sea level, a geoid, seabed, or local rig datum. <code>DateTimeUtc</code> is an
        explicit UTC instant, required so the correct point on each model's secular-variation term is used.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        The returned magnetic flux density is expressed in the local <b>north-east-down (NED)</b> frame, in SI
        teslas. <code>Declination</code> is SI radians, positive east of geodetic north; <code>Inclination</code> is
        SI radians, positive downward from horizontal. <code>HorizontalIntensity</code> and
        <code>TotalIntensity</code> are the non-negative norms of the horizontal (north-east) and full
        three-component vector. GeographicLib's own degree, east-north-up, and nanotesla conventions are confined to
        the implementation and never appear at the API boundary.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Every calculation request is stateless and atomic: input samples and results are not persisted, all samples
        in a batch are validated together, and one invalid sample rejects the complete request rather than returning
        a partial result. Only cumulative operational counters are retained by the service. The web interface can
        display and accept other units through the OSDC unit-reference system, but SI radians, SI metres, SI teslas,
        and UTC instants are always what cross the API boundary.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Using this web application</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Open <MudLink Href="/EarthMagneticField/webapp/EarthMagneticFieldCalculation">Evaluate magnetic field</MudLink>,
        choose WMM2025 or IGRF14, enter a UTC evaluation instant, a WGS84 latitude and longitude — as sexagesimal or
        in the selected angular unit — and an ellipsoidal depth, then press <i>Evaluate</i>. The input and result
        fields follow the unit system selected for the page, while the service itself always exchanges SI radians,
        SI metres, and SI teslas. The result panel reports the north, east, and down flux-density components,
        horizontal and total intensity, declination, inclination, and the identity of the model used.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The <MudLink Href="/EarthMagneticField/webapp/EarthMagneticFieldModel">Magnetic models</MudLink> page shows
        both loaded models' identity, degree/order, release date, validity bounds, and coefficient checksums. The
        <MudLink Href="/EarthMagneticField/webapp/StatisticsEarthMagneticField">Usage statistics</MudLink> page shows
        cumulative request counters retained across pod restarts when the service's persistent <code>/home</code>
        volume is retained.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4 mb-6" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Access methods</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
        The same evaluation is reachable through several interfaces built on the same stateless model, so it can be
        used interactively or wired into other systems and AI agents.
    </MudText>
    <MudSimpleTable>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Web page</b></td>
                <td>
                    This site — a server-side Blazor application (this page) for manual, one-off evaluations and for
                    inspecting the loaded magnetic models and usage statistics.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>REST API</b></td>
                <td>
                    JSON endpoints under <code>/EarthMagneticField/api/EarthMagneticField</code> for discovery,
                    model information, and synchronous batch evaluation, documented and explorable through the
                    Swagger UI at <code>/EarthMagneticField/api/swagger</code>. Suited to scripts, backend
                    integration, and any HTTP client.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>MCP</b></td>
                <td>
                    A Model Context Protocol server exposed as Streamable HTTP at
                    <code>/EarthMagneticField/api/mcp</code>, publishing three tools — connectivity <code>ping</code>,
                    <code>earth_magnetic_field_get_model_info</code>, and <code>earth_magnetic_field_evaluate</code>
                    — with strict input/output JSON Schemas, so AI agents and MCP-aware tooling can call the same
                    evaluations directly.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Reusable web pages</b></td>
                <td>
                    The Razor components behind the evaluation, magnetic-model, usage-statistics, and Home pages are
                    published as the <code>OSDC.Drilling.EarthMagneticField.WebPages</code> NuGet package, so other
                    Blazor applications can embed the same unit-aware controls instead of re-implementing this UI.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </MudSimpleTable>
</MudPaper>
